WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.880
There was a time when a computer filled a room. Today we carry one in our pocket.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:11.600
People who once waited days for a calculation can now explore a thousand

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:17.600
possibilities before lunch. Behind that progress were people who looked at what

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.160
existed and imagined something better. Imagine that computers could belong in

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:30.240
homes. Imagine that we could draw on a screen, speak across continents, or learn

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:36.280
from a library that never closes. Now artificial intelligence is expanding what

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:41.640
we can do with these machines. We can describe an idea and begin turning it

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:48.320
into code, music, images, or even a working design. For someone with curiosity and an

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.660
ordinary computer, the distance between imagining something and building it is

00:00:53.660 --> 00:01:00.800
getting shorter. That makes this an extraordinary time to be creative. But

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.800
But having the ability to make something still leaves us with a question:

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.960
What's worth making? A computer cannot experience your neighborhood as you do.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:18.080
has never sat in your classroom, struggled with your particular problem, or dream

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:25.120
your particular dream. Those experiences give you something to contribute. They

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.360
help you notice the missing tool, the overlooked person, the better way. As AI

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.920
advances we will need people willing to ask unfamiliar questions. People who can

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:42.160
bring art into engineering, compassion into design, and fresh thinking into

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.280
problems everyone else has learned to accept. We'll also need patience. The

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:54.800
willingness to test an idea, discover that it fails and work on it again. Powerful

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.040
tools can help us move faster and good judgment helps us choose where to go. So

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.520
learn how these computers work, experiment with them, build something small

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.920
that matters to someone. Let your first attempt teach you enough to make a second

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.120
the next great invention may begin with a person looking at an everyday

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:24.120
frustration and saying there has to be a better way. I, O'Brian Griffith, truly

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.280
believe that I could be that person and with the tools now becoming available I'm

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.250
ready to explore, experiment, and build.